l kinds  ethnographic modern and decorative arts  as well this slide I won't read it to  you but it begins to sort of  characterize what we're trying to  achieve in terms of critical thinking  and decision-making an understanding of  the broad range of materials that you're  likely to encounter as a conservator and  although it's for our program it's  certainly true of all the graduate  programs in the United States our  program as I said is a three-year  program of study during the first year  students are exposed to all areas of  conservation and what we call blocks so  we teach a paper blog photographic  conservation block of paintings block  and objects block where they learn about  these materials what they are how they  deteriorate how to preserve them  scientific concepts associated with  their care they learn about advocacy and  public engagement as well I teach the  photographic conservation block that's  my field and so in that  case I want to ensure that all of these  students whether they major in  photography or not have a broad  understanding of the different materials  negatives and prints by the way that's  my grandson there and digital on the on  the right but everything from the  daguerreotype to the digital print  during the last photograph conservation  blocks we've also been involved in  emergency response and preparedness the  last two years we've worked to recover  three hundred photographs one year  damaged in a fire and the following year  in a flood we believe very strongly in  the importance of community engagement  and connecting to these activities and  sharing our knowledge and skills and  learning from those experiences in very  significant ways during the second year  the students are focused in their  discipline what they're going to major  in learning about scientific concepts  about the treatment ethical decision  making epics and philosophy associated  around their majors here are just some  of my favorite most recent projects by 