ngly in  the importance of partnerships and we  have been working recently thanks to  support from the mellon Foundation with  historically black colleges and  universities building awareness about  the preservation of their photographic  and now audio-visual collections  connecting our students to their  students trying to inspire their  students to think about careers in art  conservation and around the globe as  well we've been working in Erbil Iraq  we've developed with other partners  certainly and Institute training Iraqis  and the preservation of cultural  heritage on the left and on the right is  a slide from our work in the Middle East  we're working closely with the Arab  image foundation to build awareness  around the importance of photographic  conservation this project particularly  funded by the mellon Foundation as well  and in collaboration with the Getty  conservation Institute more recently  we've been working in Africa to try and  build awareness around photographic  conservation in sub-saharan Africa here  is a course in the School of African  heritage in Baneen so finally  fundraising of course is very key in all  these efforts and very rewarding as well  and we work across the spectrum with  foundations corporations individuals  government agencies and we're grateful  to their support and all of these  activities whether they're supporting  students for research travel or stipend  support finally just a few other remarks  here it is so important in developing  this curriculum that you continue to  revise it that you ensure that you're  meeting the needs of the field in a very  powerful way that you consult with your  lumps and the field more general and  allied professionals to address a  curriculum for example we're beginning  to doing do more now and preventive  conservation to do more with new  technologies to do more with modern and  contemporary art and we need to ensure  that our students are well prepared to  meet these emerging global n